<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chapter-algebra-changing-subject">
  <title>Changing the Subject</title>
  <section xml:id="algebra-changing-intro">
    <title>Introduction</title>
    <introduction><p>A formula expresses a relationship among quantities. Changing the subject means rearranging a formula to isolate a different variable while keeping the relationship equivalent.</p></introduction>
    <p>Work with inverse operations, then substitute simple values to check that the rearranged formula is consistent with the original.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="algebra-changing-subject-practice">
    <title>Worked Example and Practice</title>
    <example xml:id="example-algebra-changing-subject">
      <title>Worked example</title>
      <statement><p>Make h the subject of A=bh.</p></statement>
      <solution><p>For b≠0, divide both sides by b: h=A/b.</p></solution>
    </example>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-algebra-changing-subject">
      <statement><p>Make r the subject of C=2πr.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>r=C/(2π).</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </section>
</chapter>
